  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Pleasant and dry conditions expected through Monday \nexcept for far eastern MA, the Cape and Islands. Elevated rip \ncurrents possible for east facing beaches Sunday. \n\nAnother cool night ahead as clear skies support radiational cooling. \nThus, opted to blend in the NBM 10th percentile and MOS guidance for \nlows tonight. Lows range from the upper 40s across elevated portions \nof the interior, to near 60 in urban and coastal areas. High \npressure moves offshore Saturday switching winds to the E with low \nrelative humidity values. Expecting another dry and pleasant day \nwith Highs across the interior likely range in the low to mid 80s \nwhile the onshore flow will keep temperatures slightly cool (mid-\nupper 70s) along the immediate eastern coastal areas.\n\nBy Sunday, heights fall as a weak trough approaches from the west. \nMost guidance shows a weak surface low tracking along a boundary \nwell to the S of the region Sunday afternoon. Compared to the last \nforecast update, the NAM has backed off on the more robust POPs \nassociated with a push of moisture across the Cape and E MA Sunday \nafternoon.  Model soundings still indicate a shallow layer of \nmoisture in the lower levels supported by the easterly surface flow \nwith drier mid-level air. This could potentially point to a layer of \nlow clouds and even some patchy drizzle in areas of eastern MA \ndeveloping through the day on Sunday. \n\nOutside of limited precip chances, there is higher confidence in a \nperiod of elevated rip current risk developing on Sunday with \nincreasing easterly flow. Greatest threat for rough surf and rip \ncurrents will likely be across much of the east-facing portion of \nthe Cape, MA, and the Islands. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic chances for showers & t-storms late Mon-Wed \nperhaps lingering into Thu...heavy rain/localized flash flood risk.\n\nThe next shortwave trough approaches the region from the west in the \nlate Mon-Wed timeframe. This will allow southerly flow to bring \ntypical summerlike humidity back to southern New England. This will \nalso bring a return to period chances of showers and thunderstorms \nto the region in the late Mon-Wed time frame...perhaps lingering \ninto Thu if the shortwave is slower to depart. While there is quite \na bit of spread in regards to timing and track...the ECMWF/GFS/UKMET \nindicating the potential for an approximately 995 mb surface low. \nThat is pretty anomalous for July standards and the EPS ensemble \nsituational awareness table indicates its strength outside the \nmodels climatology field. Pwats are also indicated to increase to 1+ \nstandard deviation above normal on the order of 1.5 to 2\". When you \ncombined that with the potential for a 995 low pressure system...the \npotential is certainly there for a heavy rain/localized flash flood \nrisk. And that may not even end up in our region depending on the \ntrack...But it certainly bears watching for the mid-Atlantic and \nnortheast states. \n\nWe also should mention that the late Mon through Wed timeframe will \nnot be a complete washout. Timing obviously uncertain at this \npoint...But afternoons/evenings are favored given diurnal \ninstability to work with as well. \n\nTemperatures fairly seasonable for this time of year. Lows may be a \nbit above normal with highs a touch below normal given the \nincreasing low level moisture  and precipitation chances for the \nmiddle of next week.",
